:"publication_history","label":"Publication History"}},{"value":"2022-10-26","order":2,"name":"accepted","label":"Accepted","group":{"name":"publication_history","label":"Publication History"}},{"value":"2023-02-07","order":3,"name":"published","label":"Published","group":{"name":"publication_history","label":"Publication History"}}]},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,30]],"date-time":"2026-07-30T14:01:35Z","timestamp":1785420095677,"version":"3.56.0"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2019,9,6]]},"group-title":"PsyArXiv","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Center for Open Science","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2019,9,6]],"date-time":"2019-09-06T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1567728000000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/legalcode"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<p>A general method is introduced in which variables that are products of other variables in the context of a structural equation model (SEM) can be decomposed into the sources of variance due to the multiplicands. The result is a new category of SEM which we call a Multiplicative Reticular Action Model (XRAM). XRAM can include interactions between latent variables, multilevel random coefficients, latent variable moderators, and novel constructs such as factors of paths and twin genetic decomposition of multilevel random coefficients. The method relies on an assumption that all variance sources in a model can be decomposed into linear combinations of independent normal standardized variables. Although the distribution of a variable that is an outcome of multiplication between other variables is not normal, the assumption is that it can be decomposed into sources that are normal if one takes into account the non-normality induced by the multiplication. The method is applied to an example to show how in a special case it is equivalent to known unbiased and efficient estimators in the statistical literature. Two simulations are presented that demonstrate the precision of the approximation and implement the method to estimate parameters in a multilevel autoregressive framework.<\/p>","DOI":"10.31234\/osf.io\/36y9n","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2019,9,6]],"date-time":"2019-09-06T00:28:32Z","timestamp":1567729712000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":1,"title":["Products of Variables in Structural Equation Models: Multiplicative Reticular Action Models"],"prefix":"10.31234","author":[{"given":"Steven M.","family":"Boker","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/0153tk833","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"University of Virginia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Timo","family":"von Oertzen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8765-6982","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Andreas Markus","family":"Brandmaier","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"15934","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,27]],"date-time":"2025-11-27T22:30:54Z","timestamp":1764282654000},"score":42.952896,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/osf.io\/36y9n_v1"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2019,9,6]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31234\/osf.io\/36y9n","published":{"date-parts":[[2019,9,6]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T02:26:24Z","timestamp":1790821584175,"version":"4.1.0"},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"American Psychological Association (APA)","issue":"1","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Psychological Methods"],"DOI":"10.1037\/a0030001","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2012,9,17]],"date-time":"2012-09-17T17:14:39Z","timestamp":1347902079000},"page":"71-86","source":"Crossref","is-referenced